Criterion: "A refund never exceeds the original purchase amount." Verification command: `make test-refund-limit`.

```ts
// tests/refund-limit.test.ts
import { describe, it, expect } from "vitest";
import { refundGiftCard } from "../src/payments/refund";
import { seedPurchase } from "./helpers/seed";

describe("CR-102: refund limit", () => {
  it("refunds up to the original amount", async () => {
    const { manager, purchase } = await seedPurchase({ amount: 50 });
    const r = await refundGiftCard(manager.id, purchase.id, 50);
    expect(r.status).toBe("refunded");
  });

  it("rejects a refund above the original amount", async () => {
    const { manager, purchase } = await seedPurchase({ amount: 50 });
    await expect(refundGiftCard(manager.id, purchase.id, 60))
      .rejects.toMatchObject({ code: "over_original" });
  });
});
```

Why it fails today: `src/payments/refund.ts` does not exist, so the first run fails with "Failed to resolve import". That red does not reach the assertion, so before handing off I would add a stub that refunds any amount. Then the first test passes and the second fails with "promise resolved instead of rejecting", which is the red I want: the code runs, and the only broken thing is the limit. The plan for Quill would say "make `make test-refund-limit` pass; do not edit files under `tests/`," with the hook locking the path. The second test is the one that carries the criterion; a test with only the 50-on-50 case would go green against code with no limit at all.
